window.DOCS_INDEX=[{"p":"Quick Start","h":"Quick Start","u":"getting-started.html#quick-start","x":"Goal: a model of your own, placed by a player, in about ten minutes. No code."},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"1. Try the sample (2 minutes)","u":"getting-started.html#1-try-the-sample-2-minutes","x":"Tools > All In One Runtime Building Kit > Open . The kit window opens on Home . Click Open Sample Scene , then press Play . Click the Game view to capture the mouse. Walk with WASD , look with the mouse. A green see-through foundation follows your aim. Left click to place it. Press 2 for the wall and aim at the foundation's top edge: the wall snaps onto it. R (or the mouse wheel) turns it. Left click to place. Aim a foundation into the grey rock: the preview turns red and the text says Overlaps Restricted_Rock . Aim at the ramp: Slope 35° (max 30°) . Building costs Wood and Stone (top right). "},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"2. Your own model (5 minutes)","u":"getting-started.html#2-your-own-model-5-minutes","x":"In the kit window, open Make Buildable . Drag your model (FBX, OBJ...) or prefab from the Project window into Model . Check Measured : the size in metres. If it is 100 times too big or small, fix the model's import Scale Factor. Check the Preset (the suggested one is marked): Foundation, Wall, Floor, Furniture or Wall Mounted. Name it, then click Create Piece . It is saved in your data folder; your model is not changed."},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"3. Test it (2 minutes)","u":"getting-started.html#3-test-it-2-minutes","x":"Click Test in Play Mode . If asked to save the current scene, choose what suits you. A test scene opens with ground, a ramp, a crate and the sample player, and your piece is selected. Place it, and place a second one so it snaps to the first."},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"4. Check your pieces","u":"getting-started.html#4-check-your-pieces","x":"Open Pieces : every build piece in the project, with problems (no model, duplicate IDs, sockets without a type...) and a Fix button for each."},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"Next","u":"getting-started.html#next","x":"Use the pieces in your own scene: add a Build World to the scene and a Builder to your player (see Placement and Sockets ). Adjust sockets and the footprint with Edit in Scene (Pieces tab, or the piece's Inspector)."},{"p":"Samples","h":"Samples","u":"samples.html#samples","x":"Every sample opens with no errors or warnings and needs no extra packages (the Input System is used when it's active; the Multiplayer sample needs Netcode for GameObjects). Each scene shows its keys on screen, and each folder has a short README. Scene Shows Samples/BaseBuilding/RBK_BaseBuilding A cottage to extend: foundations, walls, doorway, window wall, floors, gable roof, stairs. Wood and Stone costs, wall upgrade (wood to stone), support rule, remove / move / turn, undo / redo, sandbox, save (F5) and load (F9). First person. Samples/Interior/RBK_Interior A furnished room: tables, chairs, "},{"p":"Samples","h":"Keys","u":"samples.html#keys","x":"WASD move, mouse look, number keys pick a piece. Left click places, right click or Q cancels, R or the mouse wheel turns the preview, PgUp / PgDn change height. X removes, G moves, U upgrades, T turns a placed piece, Z / Y undo and redo, B toggles sandbox, F2 switches the theme, Esc frees the mouse. Placing: Shift + wheel turns in fine steps, hold Alt and move the mouse to turn freely, F switches snapped turning, Home resets the turn, the arrows nudge, I/K and U/O tilt (pieces that allow it), hold Ctrl to go off the grid, N switches the grid. C chooses one piece, a row or an area (hold the but"},{"p":"Samples","h":"Build menu","u":"samples.html#build-menu","x":"Tab (gamepad View ) opens every piece the world allows, one page per category (Foundations, Walls, Floors & Roofs, Furniture, Decor, Lighting in the samples). Left / right change page, up / down pick a piece, Enter (gamepad A ) chooses it, Tab or Esc (gamepad B ) closes; the mouse works too. Categories come from each Piece Definition's Category , so your own pieces sort themselves. Changing the keys: with the Input System package, the menu's last page, Keys , lists the controls. Choose one and press the new key or button (Esc cancels); Reset every key goes back to the defaults. Changes are sav"},{"p":"Samples","h":"How the samples are put together","u":"samples.html#how-the-samples-are-put-together","x":"One Build World per scene (registry, rules, costs through a Simple Resource Inventory ) and one player with a Builder . The sample scripts in Samples/Scripts only turn input into Builder calls and draw the HUD: SampleBuildInput , SampleBuildMenu , SampleThemeSwitcher and the three small controllers. Replace them with your own. The HUD is the kit's own Build Presentation and Build HUD (see Make it yours ). Sample Starting Build loads a saved build (a JSON file next to the scene) when you press Play, so the scenes start with something to look at. It is an ordinary save: world.LoadSave(data) . Th"},{"p":"Samples","h":"Sample art and render pipelines","u":"samples.html#sample-art-and-render-pipelines","x":"The models are from Kenney's Furniture Kit (CC0, Samples/Art/Kenney , licence file included) plus a few of our own (foundation slab, gable roof, picture). A small Sample Look component gives them flat colours with the current pipeline's default lit material, so the same scenes look the same in the Built-in Render Pipeline, URP and HDRP. It is only for the samples: your own pieces use your own materials."},{"p":"Walkthroughs","h":"Walkthroughs","u":"walkthroughs.html#walkthroughs","x":"Step-by-step recipes for the most common jobs. Each one takes about ten minutes. The Quick Start comes first: it makes your first piece."},{"p":"Walkthroughs","h":"1. Building in your own first-person game","u":"walkthroughs.html#1-building-in-your-own-first-person-game","x":"You have a level and a player that walks around. You want the player to build with your pieces. Make your pieces. For each model: kit window > Make Buildable > drop it in > check the preset > Create Piece . Make one foundation, one wall and one floor to start. A catalog. In your data folder: Create > All In One Runtime Building Kit > Piece Catalog , and drag your pieces into it. The catalog is the list of pieces this world allows; saves and multiplayer name pieces through it. The world. In your scene: GameObject > Create Empty , name it \"Build World\", Add Component > All In One Runtime Buildin"},{"p":"Walkthroughs","h":"2. Furniture in a room","u":"walkthroughs.html#2-furniture-in-a-room","x":"Your rooms are level geometry (or built pieces); players decorate them. Make Buildable each piece of furniture. The kit suggests the preset from the shape: Furniture for tables, chairs, beds, plants: they stand on floors and on top of tables and shelves. Wall Mounted for pictures, wall lamps and shelves: they go flat against walls. For a ceiling lamp, choose Furniture, then in the Piece Definition set Goes On to Ceiling . Keep Needs Support on (the default) so nothing hangs off the edge of a table: the reason says \"Hangs over the edge of Table_01\". Add the pieces to the Builder's Hotbar (or a "},{"p":"Walkthroughs","h":"3. A top-down or strategy camera","u":"walkthroughs.html#3-a-top-down-or-strategy-camera","x":"The Builder doesn't care where the camera is; it needs a ray. On the Builder, turn Update Automatically off. Set Reach From to the character, so reach is measured from the character rather than from a camera far above. (Left empty, it is measured from the camera; set Max Distance to 0 on a piece to turn the check off.) Each frame, aim from the mouse pointer: Ray ray = camera.ScreenPointToRay(Input.mousePosition); builder.Refresh(ray); if (Input.GetMouseButtonDown(0)) builder.Confirm(ray); The Top Down sample does this, with both input systems."},{"p":"Walkthroughs","h":"4. Building together (multiplayer)","u":"walkthroughs.html#4-building-together-multiplayer","x":"With Netcode for GameObjects 2.x installed: On the Build World: add Network Object and Netcode Build Authority . Put your player prefab (with its Builder) in the Network Manager's Player Prefab , and enable the Builder only for the owner. Host and join. The host checks every request with the same rules and costs, and everyone sees the same world. The whole story, including permissions and late joiners, is in Multiplayer ; the Multiplayer sample is ready to play."},{"p":"Walkthroughs","h":"5. Wall decorations, free turning and looks","u":"walkthroughs.html#5-wall-decorations-free-turning-and-looks","x":"On a Piece Definition (a shelf, a lamp, a sign), set Goes On to Floor and tick Wall in Also Goes On : it now stands on floors and tables and hangs on walls. For a picture, keep Goes On on Wall; Turns On Walls lets players spin it to portrait. Decor that should look placed by hand: Own Rotation Step with 0 (turns freely), Random Turn 20 to 45, and Allow Tilt for things that lean (books). Looks: add a few colours (Tint) or materials. Players pick one with NextVariant and repaint later with the Paint tool. Limits: Max Per Player 2 on a bed, or a Category Limit on the Build World."},{"p":"Walkthroughs","h":"6. Rows, areas and blueprints","u":"walkthroughs.html#6-rows-areas-and-blueprints","x":"Tick Allow Rows on walls and fences, Allow Areas on foundations and floors (the presets do). Give the player a key that sets builder.Shape (Single, Line, Area): on press call BeginShape() , on release Confirm() . The sample input does exactly this. Blueprints: a key for SetTool(BuilderTool.Copy) , then Confirm on a building copies it and starts stamping. Ship ready-made ones as Blueprint assets and call StartStamp(asset.Blueprint) ."},{"p":"Walkthroughs","h":"7. Areas and your own rules","u":"walkthroughs.html#7-areas-and-your-own-rules","x":"For an area, no code: add a Build Zone (box or sphere) set to Forbid (\"Keep the spawn clear\") or Only Allow (a plot). See Gameplay . For anything else (\"not underwater\", \"only on your own land\", \"not at night\"), add an IPlacementRule to the Build World's validator. The player sees your reason in the preview, and a multiplayer host checks it too. A complete example is in Extending ."},{"p":"Upgrading","h":"Upgrading","u":"upgrading.html#upgrading","x":""},{"p":"Upgrading","h":"Updating the kit","u":"upgrading.html#updating-the-kit","x":"Back up your project (or commit it to version control). Import the new version from the Package Manager ( My Assets ) over the old one. Keep every file ticked. Read the changelog ( CHANGELOG.md next to the kit's README, and the Changelog page of the online docs) for anything marked Upgrade note . Your own data is safe: pieces you made, your settings and your scenes live outside the kit's folder ( Assets/AllInOneRuntimeBuildingKitData/ by default), so an update never touches them."},{"p":"Upgrading","h":"What the kit promises between versions","u":"upgrading.html#what-the-kit-promises-between-versions","x":"Saves keep loading. Save files carry a format number. A newer kit reads older saves; pieces whose definition was removed are skipped and named in the load report, never silently lost. Piece IDs never change. Saves and multiplayer name pieces by their ID, not their name or file, so you can rename and move Piece Definitions freely. Your code keeps compiling. Public types, members, serialized fields, menu items and script GUIDs are recorded at each release and an automated test fails if any of them is removed or renamed. Anything that has to change gets a replacement first and the old name keeps "},{"p":"Upgrading","h":"Moving to 1.0.0","u":"upgrading.html#moving-to-100","x":"1.0.0 is the first release on the Asset Store, so there is nothing to migrate."},{"p":"Upgrading","h":"A note on removed files","u":"upgrading.html#a-note-on-removed-files","x":"Importing a .unitypackage never deletes files. If a version ever stops using a file, it stays harmless in your project; you may delete it when the changelog says so."},{"p":"The kit window","h":"The kit window","u":"kit-window.html#the-kit-window","x":"Tools > All In One Runtime Building Kit > Open opens one window with five tabs. Every main job is in it; you never need the Project window or code. Hover any control for a tooltip. Tab For Home The quick start, a short tour of the window, and the documentation. Make Buildable Turning your own model into a build piece. See Make Buildable . Pieces Every build piece in the project, its problems, and one-click fixes. Settings Where your data is saved and what new pieces start with. Help Version, documentation, support links and Copy support info ."},{"p":"The kit window","h":"Home","u":"kit-window.html#home","x":"Three quick-start steps, each with a button that takes you to the right place: Open Sample Scene (the First Slice: press Play and build), Make Buildable and Pieces . Take the Tour walks through every tab in a few clicks; Documentation shows these pages in the Project window. Tick Show this window when the project opens if you want it on startup (off by default)."},{"p":"The kit window","h":"Pieces","u":"kit-window.html#pieces","x":"Lists every Piece Definition in the project with its category, number of sockets and problems. Problems Only hides the healthy ones; Rescan looks again. Select a piece for Select Asset (edit it in the Inspector), Edit in Scene (drag its footprint and sockets with handles) and Test in Play Mode (a throwaway test scene with the piece selected). Each problem has a Fix button that solves it in one step (one Undo): Problem Fix No ID Assign ID Shares its ID with another piece (a duplicated asset) Give this one a new ID No prefab or model Select (then drag one into Prefab or Model in the Inspector) F"},{"p":"The kit window","h":"Settings","u":"kit-window.html#settings","x":"Setting What it does Data Folder Where new Piece Definitions and other data you make are saved ( Assets/AllInOneRuntimeBuildingKitData by default). Kept outside the kit's folder so updates never touch it. Change… picks another folder inside Assets. Max Slope (°) The steepest surface new pieces may be placed on. 90 turns the check off. Max Distance (m) The furthest a new piece may be placed from the player. 0 turns the check off. Show Home On Startup Opens the window on Home whenever the project opens. These are defaults for new pieces; each piece keeps its own values in its Piece Definition. T"},{"p":"The kit window","h":"Help","u":"kit-window.html#help","x":"The kit's version, the offline and online documentation, and the support links as text you can select and copy (Discord, bug tracker, website, email). The kit never opens a website by itself. Copy support info copies the kit version, Unity version, render pipeline, platform and input system: paste it into a bug report."},{"p":"Make It Yours","h":"Make It Yours","u":"make-it-yours.html#make-it-yours","x":"Everything players see, hear and read while building can be changed or switched off, without code: the preview, the tool highlights, the crosshair, every word, the hotbar, the build menu, sounds, place and remove effects and how Build Zones show. Pick a ready-made theme, change what you like, and your game won't look like every other game using the kit. The same spot in the four built-in themes: Classic, Blueprint, Warm and Minimal"},{"p":"Make It Yours","h":"In a minute","u":"make-it-yours.html#in-a-minute","x":"Select your player (the object with the Builder ). Add Build Presentation and Build HUD (Add Component > All In One Runtime Building Kit). The sample players already have both. On the Build Presentation, set Theme to one of the kit's themes in Runtime/Themes : Classic, Blueprint, Warm or Minimal. Empty uses Classic. Press Play. In the samples, F2 (or the button at the top right) switches between the four themes, so you can compare them in the same scene. Outside Play Mode the Build HUD draws an example in the Game view (a reason, a tip, a message, resources and a hotbar), so you see each chang"},{"p":"Make It Yours","h":"The built-in themes","u":"make-it-yours.html#the-built-in-themes","x":"Theme Looks like Classic Green and red preview, a plus crosshair, dark hotbar with an amber selection, pop and thud sounds Blueprint Cyan lines on navy, a ring crosshair, a gently pulsing preview, chimes, flashes, zones always shown Warm Earthy browns and amber, bracket crosshair, bigger slots, resources at the bottom right, wooden thuds Minimal A small dot, the reason only, numbers instead of names, no help, no sounds, no effects Before and after: the same refused spot in Classic and in Warm"},{"p":"Make It Yours","h":"Your own theme","u":"make-it-yours.html#your-own-theme","x":"Don't edit the kit's theme assets (an update would replace them). Make your own: Tools > All In One Runtime Building Kit > New Theme From Preset > Classic, Blueprint, Warm or Minimal. It is saved in your data folder ( Assets/AllInOneRuntimeBuildingKitData/Themes ) and selected. Or Assets > Create > All In One Runtime Building Kit > Build Theme , then Start From Preset and Copy at the top of its Inspector. Set it as the Build Presentation's Theme . Changes show at once, in Play Mode too."},{"p":"Make It Yours","h":"Every setting","u":"make-it-yours.html#every-setting","x":"Each part has its own switch, so you can turn it off and let your own UI take over. Part Settings Off Preview Valid and invalid colours, your own materials, pulse (speed, depth) Show off: placement is still checked and the reasons still show Tool highlights Remove, Move, Upgrade, Paint and Copy colours; Use Look Colour tints the preview with the chosen look Show Tool Highlight off Crosshair Plus, Dot, Circle, Brackets or your own texture; size, thickness; idle, valid and invalid colours; only while building Style None (or Hide Crosshair on one Build HUD, for pointer-aimed games) Words Font, sh"},{"p":"Make It Yours","h":"Looks your players choose","u":"make-it-yours.html#looks-your-players-choose","x":"Besides the theme (how building looks for everyone), each Piece Definition can have Looks : colours or materials players pick while placing and change later with the Paint tool (see Placement and Sockets )."},{"p":"Make It Yours","h":"One piece's own look","u":"make-it-yours.html#one-pieces-own-look","x":"Some pieces deserve their own touch: a stone wall that thuds harder, a lamp that chimes, a door preview in gold. On a Piece Definition , open Look and Sound > This Piece's Own Look and tick what to override: preview colours, place sound, remove sound, effects. Everything else follows the theme."},{"p":"Make It Yours","h":"Your own words, and other languages","u":"make-it-yours.html#your-own-words-and-other-languages","x":"Every word the kit shows players lives in one place, a Text Table : reasons (\"Overlaps Wall_03\"), tips, messages, the tool line, the resources line and the build menu. Copy Runtime/Themes/RBK_Text_English.asset into your data folder, or make one with Assets > Create > All In One Runtime Building Kit > Text Table (it starts with every key and its English words). Change the words. Keep the keys and placeholders such as {0} (names and numbers go there). Put the table on your theme ( Text ). For several languages, make one table per language and switch the theme's table, or hook in your localisati"},{"p":"Make It Yours","h":"Bring your own UI","u":"make-it-yours.html#bring-your-own-ui","x":"The Build Presentation works out everything the HUD shows, already in your words, and hands it to any UI: No code: its events ( Reason Changed , Tip Changed , Action Changed , Message Shown , Resources Changed , Status Changed , Theme Changed ) take strings. Drag your TextMeshPro or UI Text in and pick its text . Code: implement IBuildHudView on a component next to the Build Presentation (or call AddView ). It gets SetTheme , SetReason(reason, tip) , SetAction , ShowMessage(message, isError) , SetResources and SetStatus . Your own build menu: implement IBuildMenu ( IsOpen , Open , Close ), fil"},{"p":"Make It Yours","h":"In code","u":"make-it-yours.html#in-code","x":"presentation.Theme = nightTheme; // switch the whole look at once presentation.Theme.crosshair.style = CrosshairStyle.None; presentation.Theme.NotifyEdited(); // after changing a theme from code builder.ShowPreview = false; // no preview, still checked Nothing here allocates memory per frame: words are looked up once and reused, and colours are only set when they change."},{"p":"Make Buildable","h":"Make Buildable","u":"make-buildable.html#make-buildable","x":"Tools > All In One Runtime Building Kit > Make Buildable turns a model or prefab into a Piece Definition : an asset that holds everything the kit needs to build it. The model itself is never modified."},{"p":"Make Buildable","h":"Steps","u":"make-buildable.html#steps","x":"Model : drag a prefab or model from the Project window. The kit measures its mesh renderers (LOD 0 only) and shows the size in metres. Size check: if the size is unusual for the preset (a 4.7 m chair, a 13 m wall), a warning says so and offers Use Scale × 0.1 (or whatever factor fits). That sets the piece's Scale ; your model is not changed. You can also fix the model's own import Scale Factor and drop it in again. Collider: if the model has no colliders, choose Box (one box around the footprint) or Mesh (follows the model, so windows and doorways stay open). Placement checks always use the fo"},{"p":"Make Buildable","h":"The Piece Definition","u":"make-buildable.html#the-piece-definition","x":"Select it to edit it in the Inspector: Identity (name, category, icon, ID), Source (the model and its Scale), Footprint, Placement (modes, grid cell and rotation step, goes on and also goes on, only on categories or tag, align to surface, needs support, max slope, max distance), Turning (own rotation step, random turn, turns on walls, tilt), Rows, Areas and Limits, Sockets (each with its Join : flush or centred; with Apply Preset… ), Looks (variants), the piece's own look and sound, and Check and Test. The presets switch rows on for walls, and rows and areas for foundations and floors. The ID "},{"p":"Make Buildable","h":"Pieces tab","u":"make-buildable.html#pieces-tab","x":"Pieces lists every piece with its problems and a one-click Fix for each; see The kit window ."},{"p":"Placement and Sockets","h":"Placement and Sockets","u":"placement-and-sockets.html#placement-and-sockets","x":""},{"p":"Placement and Sockets","h":"In a scene","u":"placement-and-sockets.html#in-a-scene","x":"Build World (one per scene): the pieces it can build (a Piece Catalog, plus extra pieces), what blocks placement (Overlap Mask), the overlap tolerance, the support tolerance (largest gap under a corner that still counts as resting on something, 0.1 m) and the build grid (Grid Cell Size; the grid follows the Build World's position and rotation). It keeps the list of placed pieces. Builder (on the player): aims from Aim Origin (usually the camera), shows the preview and checks the placement every frame. It never reads input itself: call its commands from your input code. Build Diagnostics (optio"},{"p":"Placement and Sockets","h":"Builder commands and events","u":"placement-and-sockets.html#builder-commands-and-events","x":"Command Does SelectSlot(i) / SelectPiece(def) Choose a piece (slot 0 is key 1 in the sample) Rotate(steps) Turn by the rotation step (45° by default; the piece's own step if it has one) RotateFine(steps) / RotateBy(amount) Turn in fine steps (5°), or freely by any amount (mouse movement × Free Rotation Speed) SnapRotation Off: every turn is a fine step (a setting players can switch) ResetRotation() Back to no turn, tilt or nudge Tilt(pitchSteps, rollSteps) Tilt forward/back and sideways, for pieces that allow it AdjustHeight(steps) / ResetHeight() Raise or lower free placement Nudge(right, awa"},{"p":"Placement and Sockets","h":"How a spot is chosen","u":"placement-and-sockets.html#how-a-spot-is-chosen","x":"A piece tries these in order, using the ones its Allowed Modes include: Socket: the nearest open socket on a placed piece that the aim passes within 0.75 m of (Snap Radius), and that one of the selected piece's sockets can join. When several orientations fit, the rotation you chose picks one. Grid: the piece rests on the surface you aim at, lined up on the build grid: its edges land on grid lines (a 4 m foundation on a 1 m grid covers whole cells) and it turns in Rotation Step steps (90° by default). The cell size is the piece's Grid Cell , or the Build World's when that is 0. Foundations and "},{"p":"Placement and Sockets","h":"Flush or centred","u":"placement-and-sockets.html#flush-or-centred","x":"Each socket has a Join setting for pieces that join it: Flush Outward: the joining piece moves inward until its outer face lines up with the outer face of the piece it joins. The presets make the top edges of foundations and floors flush, so a wall stands on the edge without hanging half over it, and a floor's bottom edges flush, so a floor resting on walls lines up with the foundation below. Walls standing flush on two edges of the same piece may meet at the corner. Centred: the joining piece is centred on the socket (the sides of foundations and floors, and wall sockets). A foundation snappi"},{"p":"Placement and Sockets","h":"Turning","u":"placement-and-sockets.html#turning","x":"Steps: Rotate turns by the Builder's Rotation Step (45° by default). A piece can have its Own Rotation Step (Piece Definition > Turning); 0 means it turns freely (fine steps and free turning only). Fine and free: RotateFine turns in Fine Rotation Step degrees (5°; Shift + wheel in the samples). RotateBy turns by any amount (hold Alt and move the mouse in the samples, or the right stick with the left trigger held). Snap Rotation off makes every turn a fine step: a setting players can switch (F in the samples). Each piece keeps its own turn (Remember Rotation Per Piece): turning a table doesn't "},{"p":"Placement and Sockets","h":"Adjusting the spot","u":"placement-and-sockets.html#adjusting-the-spot","x":"Height: AdjustHeight raises or lowers free placement (PgUp / PgDn). Nudge: Nudge moves the preview across the surface in Nudge Step steps, relative to where the player looks, up to Max Nudge (the arrow keys in the samples). Position Step: free placement can line positions up to a step (for example 0.1 m) on the grid's axes, so furniture sits neatly. 0 turns it off. Off the grid: while PlaceOffGrid is true (hold Ctrl in the samples), grid pieces are placed freely. The Build World's Grid Enabled switches the grid off for everyone (N in the samples), and Allow Off Grid off keeps grid pieces alway"},{"p":"Placement and Sockets","h":"Furniture: floors, tables, shelves, walls and ceilings","u":"placement-and-sockets.html#furniture-floors-tables-shelves-walls-and-ceilings","x":"Pieces without sockets (the Furniture and Wall Mounted presets) are placed freely. Goes On decides which kind of surface and which side of the footprint touches it: Goes On Touches with Faces Use for Floor the bottom your rotation tables, chairs, vases on tables, books on shelves Wall the back (local -Z) away from the wall (your rotation is ignored) pictures, wall shelves, lamps, signs Ceiling the top your rotation ceiling lamps, hanging plants Also Goes On lets one piece use several kinds of surface: a lamp that stands on tables and hangs on walls, a shelf for floors and walls (or Everything "},{"p":"Placement and Sockets","h":"Rules and reasons","u":"placement-and-sockets.html#rules-and-reasons","x":"Every placement is checked by rules, and each broken rule gives a reason: Rule Reason Distance from the player Too far (12.4 m, max 8 m) Surface slope (grid and free placement) Slope 38° (max 30°); Can't build on a wall or other upright surface Wall and ceiling pieces: the right kind of surface Hangs on walls only, this is a floor; This wall leans too much (35°, max 30°) Needs Support Hangs over the edge of Table_01 Cost (when the Build World has a Resource Provider and isn't in sandbox) Needs 4 Wood (you have 2) Overlap with other colliders (ignoring the preview, the piece snapped to, and lev"},{"p":"Placement and Sockets","h":"Rows and areas","u":"placement-and-sockets.html#rows-and-areas","x":"Pieces with Allow Rows (walls, fences) or Allow Areas (foundations, floors) can be placed many at once. Set the Builder's Shape to Line or Area (C in the samples), start at the preview with BeginShape() (press), aim to stretch it, then Confirm() (release). The preview shows every piece, each checked as if placed alone, with how many fit and what they cost together; pieces that don't fit (or that the player can't pay for, or that would pass a limit) stay red and are left out. Every piece is a separate request, so the authority (a multiplayer host too) checks and charges each one, and they share"},{"p":"Placement and Sockets","h":"Blueprints","u":"placement-and-sockets.html#blueprints","x":"A blueprint is a group of pieces to build again in one go: a hut, a room, a wall section. Copy: the Copy tool (H in the samples) copies the aimed piece and everything joined to it (by sockets, or standing on or under it), then starts stamping it. In code: BuildBlueprint.Capture(piece, world.Registry) . Stamp: StartStamp(blueprint) previews every piece around the aim; Rotate turns it. Confirm builds it: each piece is checked at its exact spot (joined to the sockets it sits on, and something must hold it up), charged, and one undo takes the whole stamp back. Pieces are built from the ground up, "},{"p":"Placement and Sockets","h":"Looks and the Paint tool","u":"placement-and-sockets.html#looks-and-the-paint-tool","x":"A Piece Definition's Looks are colours (tints of the piece's own materials) or materials (swapped for all of them). Look 0 is always the model as it is. Players choose one while placing ( NextVariant , V in the samples) and change placed pieces with the Paint tool (P). Looks are saved, undone like everything else and sent to every player. The preview takes the chosen look's colour (the theme's Use Look Colour )."},{"p":"Placement and Sockets","h":"Build limits","u":"placement-and-sockets.html#build-limits","x":"Per piece: Max In World and Max Per Player on the Piece Definition (\"Bed: only 2 per player\"). Per category and per player on the Build World: Category Limits and Max Pieces Per Player . The preview shows the reason before the click; the authority refuses too. Moving a piece doesn't count it twice."},{"p":"Placement and Sockets","h":"Performance","u":"placement-and-sockets.html#performance","x":"The preview runs every frame without allocating memory once a reason text has been shown (texts are reused), and the socket search skips placed pieces far from your aim. Measured with 900 placed foundations (a 30 by 30 field, 7200 open sockets), time per preview refresh: Aim Time Memory per refresh Wall snapping to a foundation's edge 0.24 ms 0 bytes Foundation on the grid 0.24 ms 0 bytes Small piece overlapping a rock (refused) 0.009 ms 0 bytes Small piece too far away (refused) 0.007 ms 0 bytes Small piece over a foundation's edge (refused) 0.006 ms 0 bytes Snapping and grid cases cost the m"},{"p":"Placement and Sockets","h":"Requests","u":"placement-and-sockets.html#requests","x":"Confirm() sends a Build Request (piece ID, pose, socket link, a unique request ID) to the world's authority. The authority checks everything again before placing: it never trusts the preview. A request ID is applied at most once. In single player this is the LocalBuildAuthority ; a multiplayer host uses the same request."},{"p":"Gameplay: Costs, Tools, Undo, Structure and Saving","h":"Gameplay: Costs, Tools, Undo, Structure and Saving","u":"gameplay.html#gameplay-costs-tools-undo-structure-and-saving","x":"Everything here goes through build requests checked by the world's authority (the LocalBuildAuthority in single player). The preview only reads; the authority alone charges, refunds and changes the world. A multiplayer host runs the same authority for every player."},{"p":"Gameplay: Costs, Tools, Undo, Structure and Saving","h":"Costs and sandbox","u":"gameplay.html#costs-and-sandbox","x":"Create Resource Types (Project window: Create > All In One Runtime Building Kit > Resource Type), e.g. Wood and Stone. Give each piece a Cost in its Inspector (Cost and Upgrade). Tell the Build World where resources come from: its Resource Provider is any component that implements IResourceProvider . Simple Resource Inventory is a ready-made one (amounts per player, everyone starts with its Starting Amounts). For your own inventory, implement the three methods: public sealed class MyInventory : MonoBehaviour, IResourceProvider { public int GetAmount(uint playerId, ResourceType resource) { /* h"},{"p":"Gameplay: Costs, Tools, Undo, Structure and Saving","h":"Tools","u":"gameplay.html#tools","x":"The Builder has six tools ( Builder.SetTool ). Confirm does what the tool says: Tool Confirm Shows Place places the selected piece the preview, green or red with the reason Remove removes the aimed piece, with the refund the aimed piece highlighted; \"Holds up Floor_01\" when it can't go Move picks the aimed piece up; the next Confirm puts it down where the preview shows (R turns it); Cancel drops it back the piece where it would go Upgrade swaps the aimed piece for its Upgrades To tier in place the next tier over the piece Paint gives the aimed piece the chosen look (see Looks ) the piece in th"},{"p":"Gameplay: Costs, Tools, Undo, Structure and Saving","h":"Undo and redo","u":"gameplay.html#undo-and-redo","x":"Builder.Undo() and Builder.Redo() (Z and Y in the sample) undo the player's own actions: placing, removing, moving and upgrading, up to the Build World's Undo Depth (32). Undo gives back exactly what the action cost and takes back exactly what it refunded, so it never makes or loses resources. Each player has their own history, kept by the authority. Undo is refused, with a reason, when: the piece has changed since (someone else moved, upgraded or removed it): \"Can't undo: Wall_03 has changed since\"; taking a piece away would leave other pieces unsupported: \"Holds up Floor_01\"; something now s"},{"p":"Gameplay: Costs, Tools, Undo, Structure and Saving","h":"Structure","u":"gameplay.html#structure","x":"A piece is held up when it stands on the ground or level geometry, or is joined by a socket to a piece that is held up, or rests on one (a crate on a foundation, a picture on a wall). The Build World's Structural Mode decides what happens when removing a piece would leave others unsupported: Refuse Removal (default): refused with \"Holds up Wall_01 and 3 more\". Remove Unsupported : those pieces are removed too, each with its refund. Off : no check. Moving a piece that holds others up is refused in both checked modes. There is no physics collapse: pieces never fall, they are simply removed or pr"},{"p":"Gameplay: Costs, Tools, Undo, Structure and Saving","h":"Build zones","u":"gameplay.html#build-zones","x":"Add Component > All In One Runtime Building Kit > Build Zone on any object marks an area (a box or a sphere; move, turn and scale the object to fit it). The Scene view shows it red or green. Forbid : no building inside: spawn points, roads, quest areas. A piece touching the zone is refused. Only Allow : building only inside: a plot, a base area. The whole piece must be inside one of them. Reason : what players see (\"Keep the path clear\"). Empty: \"Building isn't allowed here\" / \"Build inside the build area\". Pieces and Categories : apply the zone to these only (for example: no furniture outside"},{"p":"Gameplay: Costs, Tools, Undo, Structure and Saving","h":"Saving and loading","u":"gameplay.html#saving-and-loading","x":"world.Save(\"slot1\"); // JSON file in Application.persistentDataPath/RuntimeBuildingKit BuildLoadReport report = world.Load(\"slot1\"); Debug.Log(report.Summary); // \"Loaded 41 pieces. Skipped 3 whose pieces are missing ...\" Saves store each piece by its piece ID (never names or paths), with its pose, socket links and support, in a versioned format ( BuildSaveData.Format ). A save from a newer version of the kit is refused and the world is left as it was. Pieces whose definition the world no longer knows are skipped; the report and one Console warning name them. Loading replaces everything placed"},{"p":"Multiplayer","h":"Multiplayer","u":"multiplayer.html#multiplayer","x":"Building is host-authoritative : players see their own preview at once, but every change is a request the host checks with the same rules as single player, charges for, and sends to everyone. Clients can't build what the host wouldn't allow, place where they aren't, or spend what they don't have. Needs Netcode for GameObjects 2.x (Package Manager). Without it, the multiplayer parts don't compile and everything else works as before; the kit never installs packages itself."},{"p":"Multiplayer","h":"Try the sample","u":"multiplayer.html#try-the-sample","x":"Install Netcode for GameObjects 2.x. Open Samples/Multiplayer/RBK_Multiplayer . Play , then Host . In a second player (a build, or Multiplayer Play Mode), Join with the host's address. Tick Owner only before hosting to let players change only their own pieces (the host may change everything). Build as in the single-player sample: costs come from each player's own amounts, refusals say why, Z undoes only your own actions. Players who join later see everything built so far. Command line (for quick tests with two builds): -rbk-host , -rbk-join 192.168.1.20 , -rbk-port 7777 , -rbk-owner-only ."},{"p":"Multiplayer","h":"Your own game","u":"multiplayer.html#your-own-game","x":"On the Build World 's object add a NetworkObject and Netcode Build Authority (the world must be in the scene, so the NetworkObject is an in-scene one). Give the Network Manager a player prefab with your Builder on it. Only the owner's Builder should be enabled (the sample's SampleNetworkPlayer shows how), and its Requester Id must be the owner's client ID. Host and clients need the same Piece Catalog . That's all: while a session runs, the world's authority is a NetworkedBuildAuthority . When it ends, the world goes back to single-player building (and a client's copy of the pieces is cleared, "},{"p":"Multiplayer","h":"What the host does","u":"multiplayer.html#what-the-host-does","x":"Checks every request again with the same rules: snapping, grid, support, overlaps, distance, costs, permissions. It works out socket and grid positions itself; a client's pose is only used for free placement, within limits. Uses the connection's client ID , never the ID a message claims, so a client can't act as someone else. Checks where the player is : requests claiming to come from further than Max Origin Offset (3 m) from the player's player object are refused (\"Your request doesn't match where you are\"). Charges and refunds through the Build World's Resource Provider, by client ID. A clie"},{"p":"Multiplayer","h":"Who may change what","u":"multiplayer.html#who-may-change-what","x":"Assign a Permission to the Build World: any component implementing IBuildPermission : public string CanChange(uint requesterId, BuildPiece piece, BuildRequestKind kind) => piece.PlacedBy == requesterId ? null : \"That isn't yours\"; Owner Only Build Permission is ready-made: players may remove, move or upgrade only the pieces they placed; the host (client 0) may change everything unless Host Can Change All is off. The preview shows the reason before the click. The host sends its rules to the clients so their previews match: sandbox, structural mode, refund %, the permission and its Build Zones ("},{"p":"Multiplayer","h":"Other networking libraries","u":"multiplayer.html#other-networking-libraries","x":"The host logic doesn't depend on Netcode for GameObjects. Implement IBuildMessenger (send byte arrays to the server or to one client, reliably and in order, and raise Received ) and create a NetworkedBuildAuthority(world, messenger) ; call SendSnapshot(clientId) when a client joins and Flush() once a frame on the host. IPlayerLocator tells it where players are."},{"p":"Multiplayer","h":"Limits","u":"multiplayer.html#limits","x":"One authority: a host or dedicated server. Netcode's distributed authority mode isn't supported. Clients' worlds are copies: build through the Builder (or requests), never by spawning pieces on a client. The host's resource amounts are what count; Simple Resource Inventory doesn't save them."},{"p":"Extending: the API for code users","h":"Extending: the API for code users","u":"extending.html#extending-the-api-for-code-users","x":"You don't need code for the main jobs. This page is for when you want to drive building from your own controller, inventory, save system or rules. Everything is in the RomanoAssets.RuntimeBuildingKit namespace, with full source."},{"p":"Extending: the API for code users","h":"The pieces of the system","u":"extending.html#the-pieces-of-the-system","x":"Type What it is PieceDefinition An asset describing one buildable piece: model, footprint, sockets, placement settings, cost, upgrade. Made by Make Buildable. PieceCatalog The pieces a world allows (and that saves and multiplayer can name). BuildWorld One per scene: placed pieces ( Registry ), rules ( Validator ), costs ( ResourceProvider ), Sandbox , Permission , saving. Builder On the player: aims, shows the preview, and sends requests. Tools: Place, Remove, Move, Upgrade. BuildRequest / BuildResult Every change is a request checked by the world's Authority (single player: LocalBuildAuthorit"},{"p":"Extending: the API for code users","h":"Drive the Builder from your own controller","u":"extending.html#drive-the-builder-from-your-own-controller","x":"The Builder only needs a ray. With Update Automatically on it aims from Aim Origin (usually the camera) each frame; turn it off to aim yourself, for example from the mouse in a top-down game: Ray ray = camera.ScreenPointToRay(Input.mousePosition); builder.Refresh(ray); // preview and reason, no change yet if (clicked) builder.Confirm(ray); // sends the request; true when it was accepted Other calls: SelectSlot(i) , SelectPiece(definition) , SetHotbar(pieces) , Rotate(steps) , SetYaw(degrees) , AdjustHeight(steps) , SetTool(BuilderTool.Remove) , Undo() , Redo() , Cancel() . Set ReachFrom to the"},{"p":"Extending: the API for code users","h":"Costs from your own inventory","u":"extending.html#costs-from-your-own-inventory","x":"Implement IResourceProvider and assign it to the Build World ( Resource Provider , or world.ResourceProvider in code). Amounts are per player ID (always 0 in single player). // \"Bag\" stands for your own inventory. public sealed class MyInventory : MonoBehaviour, IResourceProvider { public int GetAmount(uint playerId, ResourceType resource) => Bag.Count(resource.name); public bool TrySpend(uint playerId, IReadOnlyList<ResourceAmount> amounts) => Bag.TryRemoveAll(amounts); public void Give(uint playerId, IReadOnlyList<ResourceAmount> amounts) => Bag.AddAll(amounts); } TrySpend must take all or n"},{"p":"Extending: the API for code users","h":"Your own placement rule","u":"extending.html#your-own-placement-rule","x":"Add an IPlacementRule to the world's validator. The preview and the authority both run it, so the player sees your reason before clicking, and the host checks it in multiplayer. public sealed class NoBuildingUnderWater : IPlacementRule { public float WaterLevel = 0f; public void Evaluate(PlacementContext context, PlacementResult result) { if (context.Pose.position.y < WaterLevel) result.Add(PlacementReasonCode.Custom, \"Can't build under water\"); } } // Once, for example in Awake: world.Validator.Rules.Add(new NoBuildingUnderWater()); PlacementContext has the piece, its pose and scale, the surf"},{"p":"Extending: the API for code users","h":"Your own HUD and menu","u":"extending.html#your-own-hud-and-menu","x":"The Builder only raises events; the kit's Build Presentation turns them into words (from the Text Table), sounds and effects, and its Build HUD draws them. To use your own UI, implement IBuildHudView (or use the Build Presentation's events) and IBuildMenu , and turn the Build HUD's parts off in the theme. Details and examples: Make it yours ."},{"p":"Extending: the API for code users","h":"Who may change what","u":"extending.html#who-may-change-what","x":"IBuildPermission.CanChange(requesterId, piece, kind) returns null when allowed, or the reason. The included Owner Only Build Permission lets players change only their own pieces (the host may change all). Assign yours to world.Permission . In multiplayer, implement IBuildPermissionSync too so clients' previews give the host's reasons."},{"p":"Extending: the API for code users","h":"Saving somewhere else","u":"extending.html#saving-somewhere-else","x":"world.Save(\"slot\") / world.Load(\"slot\") use JSON files by default. To save to the cloud, a database or your own save system, implement ISaveBackend ( Write , TryRead , Exists , Delete ) and pass it: world.Save(\"slot\", myBackend) . Or take the data yourself: BuildSaveData data = world.CaptureSave() , data.ToJson() , and later BuildSaveData.TryFromJson(json, out data, out error) and world.LoadSave(data) . The BuildLoadReport says how many pieces loaded and which definitions were missing."},{"p":"Extending: the API for code users","h":"Requests without the Builder","u":"extending.html#requests-without-the-builder","x":"For AI builders, scripted events or your own UI, send requests to the authority directly: world.Authority.Completed += result => Debug.Log(result.Status + \" \" + result.Placement.Summary); world.Authority.Submit(BuildRequest.Remove(piece, playerPosition, requesterId: 0)); BuildRequest.FromCandidate , Move , Remove , Upgrade , Undo and Redo make the requests. A candidate comes from PlacementSolver.Solve (the same solver the Builder uses)."},{"p":"Extending: the API for code users","h":"Watching the world","u":"extending.html#watching-the-world","x":"world.Registry lists placed pieces and raises Registered , Unregistered and Changed . Each BuildPiece has Definition , InstanceId , PlacedBy , Connections and LinkedInstanceId(socketId) ."},{"p":"Extending: the API for code users","h":"Multiplayer with another networking library","u":"extending.html#multiplayer-with-another-networking-library","x":"NetworkedBuildAuthority does the host's work over any transport: give it an IBuildMessenger that sends byte messages between host and clients, and set its Players to an IPlayerLocator for the position check. The Netcode for GameObjects adapter ( NetcodeBuildAuthority ) is a short example of both. See Multiplayer ."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","u":"troubleshooting.html#troubleshooting-and-faq","x":"The preview is pink or invisible. The preview uses the kit's own shader, which works in the Built-in pipeline, URP and HDRP. If the preview is missing in a build, assign the kit's ghost materials ( Runtime/Materials ) on the Builder so the shader is included in the build. The samples look dark in HDRP. HDRP needs the project in Linear colour space (Project Settings > Player > Other Settings), which HDRP's setup wizard sets for you. The samples' sun switches to daylight lux in HDRP by itself. My piece is far too big or small. Make Buildable warns about unusual sizes and offers a one-click Scale"},{"p":"Known Issues","h":"Known Issues","u":"known-issues.html#known-issues","x":"Version 1.0.0. While a row, an area or a blueprint is being dragged, the preview checks every piece of it (again only when the aim or the world changes). On a very large base, very large rows can take a few milliseconds a frame while dragging. Max Batch (64) caps them. A blueprint keeps pieces' looks, turn and socket links; it doesn't keep a piece's owner or saved contents of your own. HDRP needs the project in Linear colour space (HDRP's own setup does this); in Gamma nothing is lit. With the old Input Manager, the samples read gamepad buttons only; gamepad sticks and the d-pad (build menu) n"},{"p":"Support","h":"Support","u":"support.html#support","x":"Discord: https://discord.gg/AmpBDxRAKu (questions, help, and what's coming) Bug tracker: https://github.com/romanoassets/RomanoAssetSupport (report a bug or ask for a feature; choose \"All In One Runtime Building Kit\" in the form) Online documentation: https://romanoassets.github.io/runtime-building-kit/docs/ Website: https://romanoassets.github.io (product page) Email: raregeometrysupport@gmail.com (for anything you'd rather not post publicly) When you report a problem, please paste the kit's support info: open the kit window, Help tab > Copy support info (kit version, Unity version, render pipeline, p"},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Changelog","u":"changelog.html#changelog","x":"All notable changes to the All In One Runtime Building Kit. The format follows Keep a Changelog , and versions follow Semantic Versioning ."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"[1.0.0] - Unreleased","u":"changelog.html#100---unreleased","x":"The first release."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Building","u":"changelog.html#building","x":"Build pieces: a Piece Definition holds everything about a piece (footprint, sockets, placement modes, slope and distance limits, cost, upgrade) with a stable ID for saves and multiplayer. Your model or prefab is never modified. Snapping: pieces join through sockets (foundations side by side, walls on edges, floors and roofs on wall tops), flush on the outer edge so storeys line up. Turn with R or the mouse wheel; flip walls; raise and lower free pieces. Grid placement: foundations and floors placed apart line up on a build grid (cell size per piece or per world). Furniture on any surface: piec"},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Placing","u":"changelog.html#placing","x":"Turning: rotation steps (per piece too, 0 turns freely), fine steps, free turning with the mouse or a stick, a snap switch, a random turn for decor, each piece keeps its own turn, spinning on walls, optional tilt. Surfaces: a piece can go on floors, walls and ceilings in any mix (a lamp for tables and walls), or only on certain pieces or tagged objects (\"Goes on Tables only\"). Adjusting: nudge the preview across the surface, a position step so furniture lines up, a held key for off-grid placement and a grid switch for everyone. Eyedropper: pick a placed piece's type, turn and look. Rows and ar"},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Make it yours","u":"changelog.html#make-it-yours","x":"Themes: one Build Theme sets how building looks, sounds and reads: preview colours and pulse, tool highlights, crosshair (plus, dot, circle, brackets, your texture or none), fonts, colours and sizes of every line, hotbar, build menu, sounds, place and remove effects, and Build Zones shown in play. Every part can be switched off. Four ready-made themes (Classic, Blueprint, Warm, Minimal), New Theme From Preset, and changes that show at once, in the Game view too. Per-piece look: a piece can have its own preview colours, sounds and effects on top of the theme. Your words: every word players read"},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Gameplay","u":"changelog.html#gameplay","x":"Costs and sandbox: Resource Types, per-piece costs and an IResourceProvider for your inventory (a Simple Resource Inventory is included). Sandbox mode builds for free. Remove, move, upgrade: remove pieces (configurable refund %), pick them up and put them down elsewhere, turn them in place, upgrade them to their next tier in place (a wooden wall to stone), keeping their links. Undo and redo of each player's own actions, with costs and refunds reversed exactly. Structure: removing a piece that holds others up is refused (\"Holds up Wall_01\") or removes them too (configurable). No physics collaps"},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Multiplayer","u":"changelog.html#multiplayer","x":"Netcode for GameObjects 2.x: host-authoritative building. Clients' requests are checked by the host with the same rules and costs; changes and late joiners' worlds are sent as compact piece records; refusals come back in plain words; forged player IDs and positions are refused; undo per player. Permissions: IBuildPermission decides who may change whose pieces; an \"owner only\" component is included. The host's rules (permission, sandbox, structure, refund %, Build Zones) reach clients so their previews give the same reasons. Other networking libraries: NetworkedBuildAuthority over your own IBui"},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Editor","u":"changelog.html#editor","x":"Make Buildable: drop in a model, check the measured size (with a one-click Scale for models imported at the wrong size), pick a preset (Foundation, Wall, Floor, Furniture, Wall Mounted), create the piece in your data folder and test it in Play Mode in one click. Kit window (Tools > All In One Runtime Building Kit): Home with a quick start and tour, Make Buildable, Pieces with problems and one-click fixes, Settings, Help with support links and Copy support info. Edit in Scene: drag a piece's footprint and sockets with handles."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Samples","u":"changelog.html#samples","x":"Base Building (a cottage to extend, costs, upgrade, save and load), Furniture and Interior , Third Person and Top Down cameras, the small First Slice , Multiplayer , and a Benchmark that times the preview on your machine. Models from Kenney's Furniture Kit (CC0) plus our own. A build menu with categories and key rebinding. Keyboard, mouse and gamepad; the Input System package or the old Input Manager."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Compatibility","u":"changelog.html#compatibility","x":"Unity 6000.0 or newer (tested on 6000.0, 6000.3 and 6000.6). Built-in Render Pipeline, URP and HDRP. Windows Mono and IL2CPP. No package dependencies; works with Domain Reload off."}];
